To achieve this setup using Terraform, you'll need to create multiple resources for your EC2 instances and configure them accordingly. Here's an outline of the steps involved:

1. Define variables: Create input variables for the number of instances (in this case, 10) and any other necessary configurations.

```hcl
variable "instance_count" {
  description = "Number of EC2 instances"
  type        = number
  default     = 10
}
```

2. Create security group: Set up a security group to allow inbound traffic from specific ports or sources as needed.

```hcl
resource "aws_security_group" "web_scraping_sg" {
  name_prefix   = "web-scraping-sg-"
  description   = "Security group for web scraping instances"

  ingress {
    from_port   = 80
    to_port     = 80
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }

  egress {
    from_port   = 0
    to_port     = 0
    protocol    = "-1"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}
```

3. Create subnet(s): If not already created, define subnets where the EC2 instances will be launched.

4. Launch EC2 instances: Use `count` to launch multiple instances based on the defined variable.

```hcl
resource "aws_instance" "web_scraping_instances" {
  count             = var.instance_count
  ami               = "ami-xxxxxxxxxxxxx" # Replace with your desired AMI ID
  instance_type     = "t2.micro"          # Choose appropriate instance type
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.web_scraping_sg.id]
  subnet_id         = aws_subnet.your_subnet_id.id

  tags = {
    Name = "Web Scraping Instance ${count.index + 1}"
  }
}
```

5. Assign Elastic IPs: Allocate and associate Elastic IPs to each instance.

```hcl
resource "aws_eip" "web_scraping_eips" {
  count = var.instance_count

  vpc      = true
}

resource "null_resource" "assign_eip_to_instance" {
  count = var.instance_count

  triggers = {
    instance_id = aws_instance.web_scraping_instances[count.index].id
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = <<EOT
      aws ec2 associate-address --instance-id ${aws_instance.web_scraping_instances[count.index].id} --allocation-id $(aws ec2 describe-addresses --filters "Name=public-ip,Values=${element(split(",", aws_eip.web_scraping_eips.*.public_ip), count.index)}" --query 'Addresses[0].AllocationId' --output text)
    EOT
  }
}
```

6. Configure your Python script: Modify your Python script to bind to the assigned Elastic IP addresses dynamically.

7. Apply changes: Run `terraform init`, `terraform plan`, and `terraform apply` to deploy the infrastructure.

This setup will create ten EC2 instances with unique Elastic IPs, allowing you to run your Python scripts in parallel while binding to different IP addresses. Note that you may need to adjust the code according to your specific requirements and environment.

