Yes, in Node.js, you can implement a pull-based stream by using the `stream` module and creating a custom readable stream that emits data on demand.

A pull-based stream allows consumers to request data from the stream when they need it, rather than having the stream push data to the consumer as it becomes available.

Here's an example of how you might create a simple pull-based stream:

```javascript
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class PullBasedStream extends Readable {
  constructor(options) {
    super(options);
    this.data = ['data1', 'data2', 'data3']; // Example data array
    this.index = 0;
  }

  _read() {
    if (this.index < this.data.length) {
      const chunk = this.data[this.index++];
      this.push(chunk);
    } else {
      this.push(null); // Signal end of stream
    }
  }
}

// Usage:
const pullStream = new PullBasedStream();

pullStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  console.log(`Received: ${chunk}`);
});

pullStream.on('end', () => {
  console.log('End of stream');
});
```

In this example, the `_read()` method is called internally by the `Readable` class whenever the consumer requests more data. The `_read()` method checks if there is more data to emit and pushes it to the stream. When all data has been emitted, it signals the end of the stream by pushing `null`.

This approach gives you control over when data is emitted, allowing for a pull-based behavior.
