Here's an example of a recursive JavaScript function that retries a request up to three times before resolving the promise:

```javascript
function retryRequest(requestFunction, maxRetries = 3) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const attempt = (retryCount) => {
            requestFunction()
                .then(resolve)
                .catch((error) => {
                    if (retryCount < maxRetries) {
                        console.log(`Retrying... Attempt ${retryCount + 1}`);
                        setTimeout(() => attempt(retryCount + 1), 1000); // Retry after 1 second delay
                    } else {
                        reject(error);
                    }
                });
        };

        attempt(0);
    });
}

// Example usage:
const makeRequest = () => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        // Simulate a network request with a random success or failure
        const isSuccess = Math.random() > 0.5;
        if (isSuccess) {
            resolve("Request succeeded!");
        } else {
            reject(new Error("Request failed!"));
        }
    });
};

retryRequest(makeRequest)
    .then((result) => console.log(result))
    .catch((error) => console.error(error));
```

In this code snippet, `retryRequest` is a higher-order function that takes another function (`requestFunction`) as its argument and returns a new Promise. The inner `attempt` function handles the actual retry logic.

The `makeRequest` function simulates a network request by randomly deciding whether to succeed or fail. You can replace this with your own asynchronous request logic.

When calling `retryRequest`, you pass in the `makeRequest` function along with any desired options (in this case, we're using the default value for `maxRetries`). If the request fails, the function will automatically retry up to three times before finally rejecting the promise with the error message from the last failed attempt. If the request succeeds at any point during these attempts, the promise will be resolved with the result of the successful request.

