To directly download a webpage using its URL in the browser's console, you can use JavaScript to create an anchor (`<a>`) element and trigger a click event on it. Here’s how you can do it:

1. Open the developer tools in your browser (usually by pressing `F12` or `Ctrl+Shift+I` / `Cmd+Option+I`).
2. Go to the "Console" tab.
3. Paste the following code into the console and press Enter:

```javascript
function downloadPage(url) {
    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.href = url;
    link.download = 'page.html'; // You can change the filename here
    document.body.appendChild(link);
    link.click();
    document.body.removeChild(link);
}

// Replace with the URL of the page you want to download
downloadPage('https://example.com');
```

This script will create a temporary `<a>` element, set its `href` attribute to the provided URL, and simulate a click on it to start the download.

If you want to save the content as HTML instead of just downloading the page, you can modify the function like this:

```javascript
async function downloadPageAsHTML(url) {
    try {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP error! status: ${response.status}`);
        const htmlContent = await response.text();

        const blob = new Blob([htmlContent], { type: 'text/html' });
        const link = document.createElement('a');
        link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
        link.download = 'page.html'; // You can change the filename here
        document.body.appendChild(link);
        link.click();
        document.body.removeChild(link);
    } catch (error) {
        console.error('Error downloading the page:', error);
    }
}

// Replace with the URL of the page you want to download
downloadPageAsHTML('https://example.com');
```

This version uses the Fetch API to retrieve the HTML content of the page and then creates a downloadable file from that content. Make sure to replace `'https://example.com'` with the actual URL of the page you wish to download.

Certainly! Below is an example of how you can write a Rust program to read URLs from a file, separate YouTube URLs from others, and then use `yt-dlp` to download the YouTube URLs.

First, make sure you have the necessary dependencies installed:

1. Install `yt-dlp` if it's not already installed on your system.
2. Add the following crates to your `Cargo.toml` file:
   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   regex = "1"
   std = { version = "1", features = ["collections"] }
   ```

Here's the complete Rust code for the task:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{self, BufRead};
use std::path::Path;
use regex::Regex;

fn main() -> io::Result<()> {
    // Read the input file containing URLs
    let path = Path::new("urls.txt");
    let file = File::open(&path)?;
    let reader = io::BufReader::new(file);

    // Define a regular expression pattern for matching YouTube URLs
    let re = Regex::new(r"^https?://(?:www\.)?(?:youtube\.com/watch|youtu\.be)/.*$").unwrap();

    // Vector to store YouTube URLs
    let mut youtube_urls: Vec<String> = Vec::new();

    // Iterate over each line in the file
    for (index, line) in reader.lines().enumerate() {
        match line {
            Ok(url) => {
                if re.is_match(&url) {
                    println!("Found YouTube URL at line {}: {}", index + 1, url);
                    youtube_urls.push(url);
                } else {
                    println!("Non-YouTube URL at line {}: {}", index + 1, url);
                }
            },
            Err(e) => eprintln!("Error reading line {}: {}", index + 1, e),
        }
    }

    // Download each YouTube URL using yt-dlp
    for url in &youtube_urls {
        let command = format!("yt-dlp -o 'downloads/%(title)s.%(ext)s' {}", url);
        let output = std::process::Command::new("sh")
            .arg("-c")
            .arg(command)
            .output()
            .expect("Failed to execute command");

        if !output.status.success() {
            eprintln!("Failed to download video from URL: {}", url);
        } else {
            println!("Downloaded video successfully from URL: {}", url);
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

### Explanation

1. **Reading the Input File**: The program opens the specified file (`urls.txt`) and reads its contents line by line.
2. **Regular Expression Matching**: It uses a regular expression to identify YouTube URLs based on their common patterns.
3. **Storing YouTube URLs**: Valid YouTube URLs are stored in a vector called `youtube_urls`.
4. **Downloading Videos**: For each valid YouTube URL, the program constructs a shell command to run `yt-dlp`, which downloads the video with appropriate options (e.g., saving it with the title as the filename).
5. **Output Handling**: The program prints messages indicating whether a URL was found or not, and whether the download was successful or failed.

Make sure to replace `"urls.txt"` with the actual path to your input file containing the URLs. Also, ensure that `yt-dlp` is available in your environment where this script will be executed.

